WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:04.713
These are not MRIs.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:06.631
They’re TikToks.

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:10.135
Hundreds of custom selected TikToks.

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:11.761
Different dances.

00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:13.722
Indoors and outdoors.

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:15.515
Entirely different vibes.

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.976
But this is not to compare
trendy dances.

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:20.770
It's to help computers 
learn to see.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.566
So I want you to guess

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:27.318
why a wide range of Tik Tok videos

00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:29.029
would be useful to help

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.238
a computer to see.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:31.906
And try to remember your answer.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:34.159
So I’m giving you this context first...

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:38.288
before I explain why this one researcher
holed up in her room

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:41.624
finding 600 perfect TikToks.

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:45.170
It's not a bad dataset.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.132
You get to watch a lot of dance videos, 
but after a while...

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:51.509
believe me, you get very tired.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.720
Okay, so you hear about 
machine learning

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.055
and artificial intelligence

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:57.015
and maybe you imagine some machine

00:00:57.015 --> 00:01:00.018
just vacuuming in all the world's 
information.

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:02.854
But a better analogy is flashcards.

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:04.898
On the front, you’ve got 
the question

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.649
on the back — the answer.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.444
There are different data sets 
for different tasks.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.696
For language, it's sentences

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:14.741
the model can learn to complete by 
checking the right answers

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.493
For an image generator

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:19.329
it would be a bunch of pictures 
with definitions.

00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:22.957
These answers on the flashcards,
they are called ground truth.

00:01:23.458 --> 00:01:25.627
The computer can rely on them 
being right.

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:28.421
Now imagine all the stuff 
we want AI to do

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.007
from transcription to generating voices.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.094
If you don't have answers 
on those flashcards...

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:36.346
ground truth, they're kind of useless.

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:38.098
And just like flashcards

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.266
the more varied and better 
your ground truth

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.394
the more an AI model can 
learn and test.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:46.231
So here is the tricky part.

00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:48.149
When do you train an AI on

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:50.735
if you want it to make 
three dimensional images

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:53.822
from two dimensional ones 
with just one viewpoint?

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:55.240
It's like a puzzle, right?

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.908
I mean, you need ground truth

00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:59.828
to know if your AI or model 
is getting things right.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.539
Yasamin started with Renderpeople.

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:04.958
It's this great dataset with ground truth...

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.335
because it includes video of people

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.754
and real 3D scans to go with it.

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:12.298
So you know exactly where they are in space.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.967
An AI could look at these people

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.052
and learn how people work in 3D.

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:18.763
But there weren't enough 
flashcards there.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.558
Yasmin and her coauthor didn't have
enough backgrounds

00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:22.642
enough variety.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:24.644
We were interested in the motion.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.896
We were interested in the 
movement of people.

00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:30.024
How they appear when they move, 
and they go through different...

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.569
appearances and poses.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:35.029
That sounds a lot like a TikTok.

00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:36.906
Was that your guess why?

00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:38.158
When I first saw this paper

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.577
I thought it was going to be 
because all the TikToks

00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:42.579
were doing the same dance 
or something

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.622
but it's actually the opposite.

00:02:44.622 --> 00:02:47.750
It's because all the TikToks 
are so different.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:51.129
Yasamin knew that they'd be good 
for training since...

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.590
not only do they show people clearly...

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:56.926
they're also full of other stuff: 
backgrounds, jackets

00:02:56.926 --> 00:02:58.052
different shapes.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:01.055
To use them, Yasamin had to 
create a 3D picture.

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:02.974
She had the phone's 
point of view

00:03:02.974 --> 00:03:06.102
so she used a program to 
remove the background...

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:07.937
and another program to estimate

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:10.398
how a person was moving 
in 3D space.

00:03:10.899 --> 00:03:13.651
She ended up with 600 videos.

00:03:14.194 --> 00:03:16.029
And her program could look at one part

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:17.572
see the depth, make a prediction

00:03:17.572 --> 00:03:20.742
how the depth would look later, 
and check her work.

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:23.077
Like flipping over a flashcard.

00:03:23.077 --> 00:03:24.913
I made my own TikTok dance called

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.414
“I cried in the shower this morning”.

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:32.587
And Yasamin was able to run it
through her model

00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:34.339
to create a 3D mesh.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.134
But it gets weirder than that.

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:43.264
Do you remember this thing?

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.728
I paid Kim and Coleman 
a dollar each to do this

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:51.606
and if we used the right music, 
we would get demonetized.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:53.900
But you know, the mannequin challenge.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.278
It involved everyone from Hillary Clinton
to James Corden.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:04:01.074
It is the most James Corden-y thing imaginable.

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:03.952
But it is also a dataset.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.828
Remember the flashcards?

00:04:05.828 --> 00:04:07.372
Imagine you want computers

00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:10.041
to learn to see depth 
in real situations.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:13.836
You could set up tons of cameras
in a circle to scan everything.

00:04:14.045 --> 00:04:16.506
High, sky, straight on, 
like Renderpeople.

00:04:16.714 --> 00:04:18.383
And you'd know all the angles.

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:21.177
But then you would lose 
the real world situation.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.639
Or you can use the TikTok data
to approximate ground truth.

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.517
But they are all moving around a ton.

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.811
If everyone is trying to stay still

00:04:29.811 --> 00:04:31.938
it’s kind of like a bunch of cameras

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:34.399
taking pictures at the exact same time

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:36.776
with all the messiness of the real world 
built in.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.404
It is a really interesting ground truth.

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:42.949
So Google researchers created 
“A Dataset of Frozen People”...

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:45.576
using tons of mannequin challenge videos.

00:04:45.827 --> 00:04:48.663
2000 videos of people not moving 

00:04:48.663 --> 00:04:49.956
and that taught a model.

00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:53.084
These became flashcards 
for a model to guess

00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:56.379
if a camera moves five feet away, 
what a scene will look like.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:04:59.299
These videos included 
tons of variety

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:02.010
variety in settings, types of people, 
everything.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.469
It was gold.

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.972
It's like thousands and thousands of flashcards

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.473
for the model to learn from.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.851
I spoke to a couple of researchers

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:11.686
who... sorry. Are we—

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.521
Are we done?
Okay.

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:15.565
All right. Thanks.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:19.902
Okay. So anyway.

00:05:20.194 --> 00:05:23.031
They use the mannequin challenge 
not for training...

00:05:23.281 --> 00:05:25.783
but to kind of grade how well 
their model did.

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:29.412
It was ground truth for them 
to evaluate their experiment.

00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:33.041
One paper shows how to learn 
the geometry of a scene

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:35.168
to fill in missing parts in photos.

00:05:35.543 --> 00:05:38.588
See how here they took an image, 
painted out part of it...

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:41.174
and then they use their knowledge
of the scene

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.467
to fill it back in.

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.010
You can see the real angle here 

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.511
and their program's version here.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:47.472
One of the papers authors told me

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:49.307
the mannequin challenge helped them know

00:05:49.307 --> 00:05:51.559
that they nailed it in any location.

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:55.688
We want to make sure this works 
for an image in the wild.

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.357
To use a randomly provided image

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:59.150
and you don't know if it’s 
indoor or outdoor.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:00.651
Or you could be anywhere.

00:06:00.651 --> 00:06:03.488
It was similar for the paper
Virtual Correspondence

00:06:03.488 --> 00:06:05.698
in which researchers figured out 
how to match points

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:08.242
in 3D scenes from different angles.

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:09.952
They could put in images

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.455
predict the pose and intersecting points

00:06:12.455 --> 00:06:15.708
and match them up for everything 
from movie scenes

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:17.335
to scenes from Friends.

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:18.336
I like Friends.

00:06:18.336 --> 00:06:20.129
I watch it more than ten times.

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:21.798
And then I thought, oh, 
maybe I can use this

00:06:21.798 --> 00:06:22.882
this thing in my paper.

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.051
I screenshot screenshot
and I just run it.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:27.553
These researchers used 
the Mannequin Challenge

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.430
as ground truth to check their work.

00:06:29.847 --> 00:06:30.848
And the many backgrounds

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:32.934
in the Mannequin Challenge 
were priceless

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:35.770
because the variety put their program 
through its paces.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:40.149
Also, they checked their model against 
a Carnegie Mellon dataset too.

00:06:40.149 --> 00:06:43.236
Where people stood in a big bubble 
filled with cameras.

00:06:43.694 --> 00:06:46.989
This thing had perfect 3D scans 
of people in video.

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:50.118
It had ground truth, but the ground truth 
was still narrow

00:06:50.118 --> 00:06:52.286
because it was in this 
sphere thing.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.206
The Mannequin Challenge was in 
the real world.

00:06:55.623 --> 00:06:58.126
Mannequin Challenge was like 
a full flashcard set...

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:00.128
with answers they could check.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:01.754
Mannequin Challenge provides a nice thing

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.631
because everyone's kind of acting still

00:07:03.631 --> 00:07:05.591
and then people go around the scene

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.927
so you can kind of get these 
extreme scenarios.

00:07:09.637 --> 00:07:11.681
So what to make of all this 
weird stuff?

00:07:11.681 --> 00:07:13.599
At first I was trying to get these researchers

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:15.518
to say how creative they are

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:17.770
but they were like, 
“It's not a big deal.”

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:19.439
But I will say this.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:22.525
I highlighted the TikTok data set and
the Mannequin Challenge

00:07:22.525 --> 00:07:25.027
but those papers are also built

00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:27.321
on top of this 3D Facebook dataset...

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:30.825
created by manually putting dots 
on people's faces.

00:07:30.908 --> 00:07:33.494
And 10,000 real estate videos

00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.788
that helped computers learn 
camera positions.

00:07:36.038 --> 00:07:37.790
The machines: They are learning.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.626
But for now, we are still the teachers.

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.421
They are only as good as the flashcards.

00:07:43.421 --> 00:07:45.548
And we're the ones making them.

